@startuml
' ============================================================================
' METADATA
'   author:       TheRobotDrafts Examples
'   description:  Variant contrasting the directed-graph view of requirement
'                 traceability (req bubbles + satisfy/verify/derive edges) with
'                 the adjacency-matrix view (req x test table) for the same set
'                 of requirements, with a note on when each view is clearer.
'   usecase:      Teaching that the SAME traceability data has two canonical
'                 presentations — a graph (good for dependency tracing) and a
'                 matrix (good for coverage auditing) — and when to use each.
'   complexity:   simple
'   domain:       systems engineering / requirements (teaching variant)
' ============================================================================

title Requirements Variant - Trace Matrix vs Graph

' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' TEACHING NOTES
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' Traceability data has two common presentations:
'
'   1. GRAPH — nodes (requirements, design, tests) + directed dashed edges
'      (satisfy, verify, derive, trace).
'      CLEARER FOR: understanding WHY something exists, tracing a single
'      requirement up to its source and down to its tests, presenting the
'      model to a stakeholder, change-impact analysis on one requirement.
'
'   2. MATRIX — a grid with requirements as rows and tests (or components)
'      as columns; a cell marked X means the edge exists.
'      CLEARER FOR: coverage auditing at a glance, finding rows/columns with
'      no marks (gaps), bulk review with a large team, export to a spreadsheet.
'
' They are two VIEWS of the same underlying links. Edit one, the other changes.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

' === LEFT: the GRAPH view ===
together {
  rectangle "REQ-A\nFeature A"  as RA <<requirement>>
  rectangle "REQ-B\nFeature B"  as RB <<requirement>>
  rectangle "REQ-C\nFeature C"  as RC <<requirement>>

  rectangle "Comp-A" as CA <<component>>
  rectangle "Comp-B" as CB <<component>>

  rectangle "T-A" as TA <<testcase>>
  rectangle "T-B" as TB <<testcase>>
  rectangle "T-C" as TC <<testcase>>

  RB ..> RA : <<derive>>
  RC ..> RA : <<derive>>

  CA ..> RA : <<satisfy>>
  CB ..> RB : <<satisfy>>

  TA ..> RA : <<verify>>
  TB ..> RB : <<verify>>
  TC ..> RC : <<verify>>
}

note left of RA
  **LEFT = Graph view**
  Good for: tracing one requirement's
  lineage and coverage, explaining the
  model, change-impact analysis.
end note

' === RIGHT: the MATRIX view (same data, as a note table) ===
note right of RC
  **RIGHT = Matrix view (same links)**
  --
  | Req  | Comp-A | Comp-B | T-A | T-B | T-C |
  |------|:------:|:------:|:---:|:---:|:---:|
  | REQ-A|   X    |        |  X  |     |     |
  | REQ-B|        |   X    |     |  X  |     |
  | REQ-C|        |        |     |     |  X  |
  --
  Good for: coverage auditing, finding
  gaps (empty rows = unsatisfied /
  unverified), bulk review, export to
  a spreadsheet.
end note

note bottom of RC
  **When to use which**
  - Graph:  "Tell me the story of REQ-B." Stakeholder review.
  - Matrix: "Show me everything missing coverage." Audit / compliance.
  Both views are generated from the same underlying trace links.
end note
@enduml
